\paragraph{Filler quality.}
\textbf{Topically related filler was more harmful, not safer.} Relative to matched unrelated filler, related business clauses changed accuracy by \qualityTwoRelevantChange{} points [\qualityTwoRelevantChangeLow{}, \qualityTwoRelevantChangeHigh{}] over \qualityTwoRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the shortest tier and by \qualityFourRelevantChange{} points [\qualityFourRelevantChangeLow{}, \qualityFourRelevantChangeHigh{}] over \qualityFourRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the middle tier. The respective paired outcomes were \qualityTwoRelevantWins{} win, \qualityTwoRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityTwoRelevantTies{} ties, then \qualityFourRelevantWins{} win, \qualityFourRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityFourRelevantTies{} ties. The longest-tier estimate was \qualityEightRelevantChange{} points, but its \qualityEightRelevantFamilyCount{} families are too sparse to strengthen that conclusion.

\paragraph{Mitigation.}
\textbf{Keyword pruning backfired on the matched cases.} Full-context accuracy was \pruningFullAccuracy{}\%, compared with \pruningPrunedAccuracy{}\% after filtering, a change of \pruningChange{} points [\pruningChangeLow{}, \pruningChangeHigh{}] across \pruningFamilyCount{} families. Only \pruningWins{} case improved; \pruningLosses{} worsened and \pruningTies{} tied (Table~\ref{tab:pruning}). The mechanism follows from the probe design: the filter retains the competing clause because it repeats the question's words, while the paraphrased answer clause has less lexical overlap.

